I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and pattern first, then use the available phase-search/indexing and GSAS-II tooling to build and refine the smallest defensible multiphase model. I’ll preserve uncertainty explicitly if any phase remains ambiguous.

The chemistry points naturally toward manganese tungstate formation, but I’m treating that only as a hypothesis: the decisive checks will be peak positions, full-profile residuals, and whether any unreacted MnO/WO₃ or secondary manganese–tungsten oxide is required. Next I’m inventorying the installed crystallographic backends and extracting peak information.

The strongest ~30 observed reflections and their intensity hierarchy match wolframite MnWO₄ (monoclinic P2/c) extremely closely; Mn₃WO₆, MnO, and common WO₃ polymorphs fail at their diagnostic strongest peaks. The observed MnWO₄ lines are systematically offset from the database cell, so I’m moving to a full Rietveld refinement rather than relying on a static reference match.

A first whole-pattern fit reaches \(R_{wp}\approx 8.7\%\) with MnWO₄ alone, and the phase Bragg \(R_F\) is about 6.9%, which is already strong evidence for a single dominant crystalline phase. I found two refinement pathologies in that trial—redundant overall/phase scales and over-correlated broadening terms—so I’m tightening the parameterization before accepting the statistics or fractions.

The residual is mostly derivative-shaped around MnWO₄ peaks, characteristic of imperfect peak-position/profile modeling, rather than isolated unmatched reflections. I’m now stress-testing the one-phase conclusion by adding the chemically plausible precursor/oxide phases one at a time and refining their nonnegative scale; this provides a more defensible impurity/detection statement than visual inspection alone.

The impurity stress test found no support for MnO, Mn₃WO₆, MnO₂, or several WO₃ polymorphs, but one ambient monoclinic WO₃ model gives a reproducible fit improvement and refines to roughly 1.8 wt%. That is chemically plausible as residual precursor, so I’m validating its diagnostic peaks and refining its lattice before deciding whether it belongs in the submitted model.

The two-phase refinement is now stable with physically valid peak widths across the full scan. The result is MnWO₄ 98.14 wt% and monoclinic γ-WO₃ 1.86 wt% (normalized crystalline basis); the WO₃ assignment is anchored by its shifted diagnostic triplet near 23.45, 23.91, and 24.70° plus lines near 33.58 and 34.45°, not just by a marginal scale factor. I’m exporting the exact refined project state and documenting the formal versus systematic uncertainty.

All required deliverables are now written from the same converged `final.gpx`. I’m doing a final consistency audit: CIF parseability/compositions, profile arithmetic and finiteness, fraction normalization, and agreement between the JSON and the saved GSAS-II project.

